xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:41:No CAS. No canonical ledger, charged file, or `jc2-lean` was inspected. Moh was read as OCR text of the whole PDF plus page images of the title page and of journal p. 150 (§2). `REPRESENTATIVE` is not `FULL_ACTUAL_EXIT`. A secondary citation is not a sourced obstruction.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:43:**What this lane can deliver.** Either (i) a sourced, desk-verified curve-level non-realisation of type `(8,6,3)`, or (ii) the exact gap in the FSY/Moh lead. It does not speak to the `S_4` representation.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:45:## 1. What the pinned Moh paper actually is
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:47:Moh JRAM 340 is a paper on *Jacobian pairs* `(f,g)` with `f_x g_y - f_y g_x = 1`, and on the tree data of their roots over the Puiseux field `k⟨⟨x^{-1}⟩⟩`. Journal pagination 140–212 is 73 pages of article (74 PDF pages). Section map, from the introduction and the body:
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:51:| Intro, pp. 140–148 | JC; configuration/tree data; approximate roots `T_r^*`; plan of §§1–6 and the two appendices. JC for `deg ≤ 100` is announced as an application. |
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:57:| §6 | Minor discs; inversion (Lemma 6.2); degree-reduction (Props. 6.2–6.4). Unnumbered Theorem, p. 200: summary of major/minor disc structure under the Jacobian condition. Numerical constraints for a putative JC counterexample of degree `< 100`: `3 ≤ s ≤ 5`, `d_s ≥ 4`. |
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:59:| App. II, pp. 207–212 | Computer enumeration leaves four exceptional degree pairs `(64,48)`, `(84,56)`, `(75,50)`, `(99,66)` (table p. 202). Coefficient counts are reduced; the remaining systems are computed. Closing sentence, p. 211: “There is no counter-example of polynomials of degrees less than or equal to 100 for the Jacobian conjecture.” |
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:61:The only “polynomial curve” language in Moh is the §2 device of treating a Jacobian pair as a parametrization *over `k(x)`*. That is not FSY’s polynomial curve (a proper map `A^1 → A^2` given by `x(t), y(t) ∈ k[t]`). The two objects share approximate-root vocabulary because both sit in the Abhyankar–Moh theory; they are not interchangeable.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:63:Moh’s theorems are about Jacobian pairs. None is a statement about the existence or non-existence of a genus-0 affine curve with a prescribed `δ`-sequence.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:67:The OCR of all 74 pages was searched for: `{6,8,3}`, `(6,8,3)`, `6, 8, 3` as a sequence, “polynomial curve”, “δ-sequence” / “delta-sequence”, genus-0 parametrizations `(t^a, t^b)`, and small characteristic data with `n=6` or `m=8`.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:73:- Closing non-existence: no JC counterexample of degree `≤ 100`.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:75:Hits that do *not* exist: a theorem, proposition, example, or remark asserting that a polynomial (genus-0, one-place-at-infinity) curve cannot have `δ`-sequence `{6,8,3}` or `(6,8,3)`; any displayed coefficient calculation for degrees 6 and 8 with remainder 3; any AM-admissible `δ`-sequence of a single plane curve.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:77:The class of objects Moh *does* treat that is numerically closest — Jacobian pairs of degrees `(m,n)` with characteristic data `{n, M_1, …}` — is constrained only under the Jacobian condition, and only for `n ≤ 100` in the computer section. A polynomial curve of type `(8,6,3)` is not a Jacobian pair.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:79:**Location verdict.** The exact theorem/example treating `{6,8,3}` is *not in the pinned PDF*. The configurations-of-roots machinery does not contain this case as a specialisation that can be read off.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:81:## 3. Attribution chain (FSY → Sathaye–Stenerson → Moh)
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:83:NR §5 recorded FSY p. 602 as a *lead*, not a kill, because Moh was not on disk. Moh is now on disk. The FSY sentence, printed p. 602, is:
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:85:> Moh [11] showed that there is no polynomial curve with the `δ`-sequence `{6,8,3}`. But this is not a negative answer to the Abhyankar’s question since there is a polynomial curve `(x,y)=(t^3,t^8)` with the `δ`-sequence `{3,8}` that generates the same semigroup as above.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:87:FSY [11] is exactly the pinned paper (correct pages 140–212). FSY does not quote a theorem number, page, or displayed calculation from Moh. The same one-line attribution, with the same `(t^3,t^8)` caveat, is in FSY’s 2004 preprint.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:89:Sathaye–Stenerson, *Plane polynomial curves* (the paper FSY cites as [14]), is the source of the attribution, and it is more precise than FSY about what Moh did *not* write. Introduction:
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:91:> Moh (with Sathaye) had calculated by hand that a polynomial curve cannot represent the sequence `(6,8,3)`. […] We rechecked the example with the help of a computer […] so far without success [of extending it].
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:95:> Moh [Mo6] observed the following: If `x(t)` and `y(t)` are polynomials of degrees 6 and 8 respectively, and `F(X,Y)` is a polynomial then the Abhyankar–Moh theory plus a calculation shows that `F(x,y)` cannot have degree 3. (Since [Mo6] didn’t actually display the calculation we recently rechecked it hoping that advances in either theory or computer software would make it more amenable to exposition. It is still messy and we will not present the calculation here either.) Hence there is no polynomial curve with characteristic `δ`-sequence `(6,8,3)`.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:97:SS [Mo6] is the same title and journal, with a page typo (`340 (1983), 331–340` instead of 140–212). The mathematical claim SS attributes to Moh is exactly FSY’s claim.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:101:1. FSY’s “Moh showed” is a one-line citation of an *undisplayed* hand calculation.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:115:- Thm. 2 (Sathaye–Stenerson converse, FSY’s “Abhyankar–Moh condition”): (1) `δ_0 < δ_1`; (2) `q_k ≥ 2`; (3) `d_{h+1}=1`; (4) `δ_k < q_{k-1} δ_{k-1}` for `k≥2`; (5) `q_k δ_k ∈ ⟨δ_0,…,δ_{k-1}⟩`.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:116:- Def. 6: a polynomial curve is one with a polynomial parametrization.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:117:- Fact 3: it has a *proper* polynomial parametrization.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:132:Approximate-root shape, from FSY Thm. 3: `n_2 = n/d_2 = 6/2 = 3`, so `g_2` is monic of `y`-degree 3, and
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:141:**Our side** (NR §0–§1.1, Galindo–Monserrat Thm. 2.1 / Prop. 2.1, locked in NR). Type `(d,n)=(8,6)`, `Δ=(8,6,c)` with `c` odd, `2c ∈ ⟨8,6⟩`, `c<24`. Conversion `β_1 = d^2/gcd(d,n) − c = 32 − c`. For `c=3`: `β_1=29`, `δ_∞=14`, `δ_aff=7`, `M_∞=30`, `S_aff=⟨8,6,3⟩=⟨3,8⟩` with the seven gaps `{1,2,4,5,7,10,13}` (NR §1.1, re-listed in PB §1 for the sibling types; the `c=3` row of NR is the same listing). Leading form: `c` is the first odd `t`-degree of a remainder of `p^3 − q^4` not in `⟨8,6⟩`.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:147:| first coordinate | `x(t)` of degree 6 | `q(t)` of degree 6 |
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:148:| second coordinate | `y(t)` of degree 8 | `p(t)` of degree 8 |
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:149:| defining polynomial | monic in `y`, `deg_y=6`, `deg_x=8` | monic in `p`, `deg_p=6`, `deg_q=8` |
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:157:**What the translation does *not* do.** The semigroup `⟨3,8⟩` is realised by `(t^3, t^8)`, whose `δ`-sequence is FSY `{3,8}` (and our `(8,3)`), not `{6,8,3}`. Primitive-generator uniqueness (FSY Lemma 3): 6 is not primitive in `⟨3,8⟩`, so it need not appear in every generating `δ`-sequence. Existence of `{3,8}` is not existence of `{6,8,3}`. This is FSY’s own caveat, and it is correct. It is also why a Galindo one-place curve of type `(8,6,3)` (NR §8: local-plus-infinity, possibly positive genus) is not a polynomial curve of that type.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:159:The swap is therefore a bijection of *types of polynomial parametrizations*, and FSY’s `{6,8,3}` is exactly our `(8,6,3)`. If Moh had proved FSY’s sentence, our type would die at the curve level.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:163:SS’s reconstruction of what Moh “observed” has two steps.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:165:**(AM).** If a polynomial curve has characteristic `δ`-sequence `(6,8,3)`, then (FSY Thm. 3 + Lemma 1, or SS’s Lemma in §4) there exist `x(t)` of degree 6, `y(t)` of degree 8, and a polynomial `g_2(X,Y)` of the displayed approximate-root shape, such that `deg_t g_2(x,y)=3`. Equivalently in our coordinates: `p` of degree 8, `q` of degree 6, all odd coefficients of `p^3−q^4` of `t`-degree `> 3` vanish, and the coefficient of `t^3` does not. This step is sourced (AM / FSY Thm. 3 / NR §5) and desk-checkable. It is a *reduction to a finite system*, not a non-existence.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:167:**(Calculation).** That finite system has no solution in the proper (gcd-1) locus. This is the step SS attributes to Moh, states is not displayed in [Mo6], and states they rechecked and will not display either because it is still messy.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:169:**Ring-theoretic form of the same system** (NR §5, re-derived). An element `r ∈ ℂ[p,q]` of degree 3 exists. Degrees of `q − α r^2` that are not in `⟨3,8⟩` are gaps `{1,2,4,5,7,…}`, so `q = α r^2 + β r + γ`. Completing the square in `r` and translating `q` gives `q = r^2` without leaving type `(8,6)`. Then `r ∈ ℂ[p, r^2]`, i.e. `H(p, r^2)=r` for some `H`. The locus `r=t^3` (or `B=0` in `r=t^3+Bt+C`) forces a degree-2 cover if `p` is even, and is the `{3,8}` curve `(t^3, t^8)` if `p=t^8`; both are out of class. The remaining identity `H(p, r^2)=r` is the same finite system. No desk identity presents itself (the ROW-SWEEP `(6,4,3)` identity used coprime degrees `(3,4)` after the swap; here the coprime pair is `(8,3)`).
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:171:**A bound that does *not* kill.** SS Theorem 1 (Davenport-type): if `x,y ∈ k[t]` are nonconstant and `x^m − y^n ≢ 0`, then `deg_t(x^m−y^n) ≥ (deg x)/n · (mn−m−n)+1`. For `q^4 − p^3` with `deg q=6`, `deg p=8` this is `≥ 11`. The identical-zero case is `p=s^4`, `q=s^3` for `deg s=2`, a gcd-2 cover, out of class. The bound is on the *total* degree of `p^3−q^4`. Our `c` is the first *odd* remainder. Even leading terms of `p^3−q^4` may sit at degree `≥ 12` while an odd remainder of degree 3 survives. Using the bound as a kill of `c=3` would be a raw-remainder-degree substitution. It is not claimed.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:179:PB is a kill chain for the *`S_4` representation*, not for polynomial-curve existence (PB §0: “does **not** assert that no polynomial curve with that `δ`-sequence exists”). On the `(8,6)` row, family 3 at `g=2`, the tube-braid gate is `3 | E` with `E = e(ι) = 2 δ_aff − 29 = c − 18` (PB (4.3)). The table PB §4:
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:188:So `(8,6,3)` survives the divisibility gate. The `(8,6)` residual of that gate is `{(8,6,9), (8,6,3)}`. A curve-level non-realisation of `(8,6,3)` would remove it from every residual that assumes a polynomial curve of the type (nodal or not); it would not be implied by, and would not imply, the `S_4` status. C6 records the six AM-numerical types of the N=4 reducible residual as still numerical; it does not treat `(8,6,3)` separately.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:194:**Type `(8,6,3)` remains OPEN as a polynomial curve.** It is not killed by the pinned source.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:196:Exact gap: Moh JRAM 340 contains no statement and no displayed proof that a polynomial curve cannot have `δ`-sequence `{6,8,3}`. FSY’s one-line citation is an attribution of an undisplayed hand calculation, inherited from Sathaye–Stenerson, who record both that Moh did not display it and that their own recheck is unpublished and messy. The FSY coordinate convention *does* identify `{6,8,3}` with our `(8,6,3)` (coordinate swap of §4, checked). The AM reduction to a finite system is sourced and correct. The emptiness of that system is the missing step. It is not desk-scale, and it is not in the pinned PDF.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:206:A later CAS lane that returns `I_29=⟨1⟩` (or that every point has non-reduced `I_{DP}`) converts the type to NON-REALIZABLE as a polynomial curve, hence as a nodal residual — stronger than the representation-level gate, which this type *passes*. Until then `(8,6,3)` stays an AM-numerical type, live at both the curve level and (for `S_4`) the representation level.
xmodel/moh-check-863-grok46-20260901.md:210:**FALLACY-v2.** Flag/place/series: not in play. Carrier/attainment: FSY’s sentence and SS’s “Moh observed” are not `FULL_ACTUAL_EXIT`; the AM reduction is a floor. Floor/attainment: Davenport `deg(p^3−q^4)≥11` was not upgraded to `c≥11`. Raw remainder degree: odd/even parts of `p^3−q^4` were kept distinct. Variable/ring map: FSY `(x,y)` = our `(q,p)` is declared in §4 with generator order and Fact 4. `sat()`, pole/interior, prime-label, merge-free, target/arrival: not in play. No gap was filled by cap or analogy.
