   174	to reduce the number of coefficients to at most 12. Thus it becomes a computable problem
   175	to show that the Jacobian conjecture is valid for all polynomials of degrees less than
   176	or equal to 100. The direct computation is presented in Appendix II.
   177	     The Computer program is a routine one and will be furnished to anyone upon
   178	request. It uses 147.148 sec of a CDC 6500 and costs only $2.41.
   179	      After many years of research we feel little Information about the distribution of
   180	roots can be added to the present article. As we pointed out before, these distribution
   181	of roots correspond to the desingularization at oo. The geometric approach of de-
   182	singularization will probably add only little Information. Our Appendix II shows that the
   183	Jacobian conjecture is quite plausible. Even if it is false a counterexample may require
   184	a large number of coefficients and is thus almost uncheckable. If the Jacobian conjecture
   185	is valid, the logical sequence to the present article is to show that a part of the data
   186	collected thus far is not permissible algebraically. The arguments in Appendix II implicitly
   187	imply that the tree data deduced thus far are permissible for two individual polynomials
   188	f(x, y) and g(x, y). This is a fact. The only hope, in our opinion, is to put all these tree
   189	data in the defining equation of f(x, y) and g(x, y) over k[x] to establish their non-
   190	permissibility. However we have had limited success in this complicated task.
   191	       The materials of this article are compiled from our research during years 1971—1978.
   192	We wish to thank our friends for their encouragement. We wish to express our appreciation
   193	t o Professor D. Drasin for reading carefully part of our manuscript.
