     2	                                                     Non-proper value set and the Jacobian
     3	                                                                   condition
     4	                                                                           Nguyen Van Chau ∗
     5	
     6	
     7	                                                                                   Abstract
     8	                                                      The non-proper value set of a nonsingular polynomial map from
     9	                                                      2
    10	                                                    C into itself, if non-empty, must be a curve with one point at infinity.
    11	                                                      2000 Mathematical Subject Classification: 14 H07, 14R15.
    12	                                                      Key words and phrases: non-proper value set, Jacobian condition.
    13	
    14	                                            1. Let f = (P, Q) : C2(x,y) −→ C2(u,v) be a dominant polynomial map,
    15	                                            P, Q ∈ C[x, y] and denote J(P, Q) := Px Qy − Py Qx . Recall that the so-called
    16	                                            non-proper value set Af of f is the set consists of all point a ∈ C2 such that
    17	                                            the inverse f −1 (K) is not compact for compact neighbourhoods K ⊂ C2
    18	                                            of a. This set Af , if non-empty, must be a plane curve that each of it’s
    19	                                            irreducible components can be parameterized by a non-constant polynomial
    20	                                            map from C into C2 ( See [J]). The mysterious Jacobian conjecture (See
    21	                                            [BMW] and [E]), posed first by Keller in 1939 and still open, asserts that
    22	                                            a polynomial map f = (P, Q) of C2 with J(P, Q) ≡ const. 6= 0 must have
    23	                                            a polynomial inverse. This conjecture can be reduced to prove that the
    24	                                            non-proper value set Af is empty. In any way one may think that in a
    25	                                            counterexample to the Jacobian conjecture, if exists, the non-proper value set
    26	                                            must has a very special configure. Such knowledges may be useful in pursuit
    27	                                            of this conjecture. In [C] it was observed that the irreducible components
    28	                                            of Af in such a counterexample can be parameterized by polynomial maps
    29	                                            ξ 7→ (p(ξ), q(ξ)) with deg p/ deg q = deg P/ deg Q. In this paper we would
    30	                                            like to notice that the non-proper value set of a nonsingular polynomial map
    31	                                            from C2 into itself, if non-empty, must be a curve with one point at infinity.
    32	                                              ∗
    33	                                                  Supported in part by the National Basic Program on Natural Science, Vietnam.
