   329	                                      degrees n(v~_y) = e_y  (BI-2, BI-3);
   330	  the dicritical vertex l itself      carries, at its AFFINE points t, all the
   331	                                      other places of E at infinity, as MARKS
   332	                                      ON AN EXISTING VERTEX, split by BI-4 into
   333	                                      K_p charged and r_p(a-a_p) - K_p exchanged.
   334	```
   335	
   336	BR's objection to BE's successor — *"a curve meeting the boundary at points of
   337	`l` adds marked points on an existing vertex; no identity consumes them"* — is
   338	answered for the marks over `p` and **only** for those: the consuming identity
   339	is BI-3 (`N = W + a` read as a fibre budget) and the consuming label is
   340	`n(v~_y) = e_y`. The marks on `l'` remain marks on an existing vertex; BI-4
   341	gives them a **budget**, not a determinant identity.
   342	
   343	### 3.4 Saturation, and the determinants of the resulting branches
   344	
   345	> **THEOREM BI-5 (mark saturation; all `N`, under `(H-∞)` and `(R1)`,`(R2)`).**
   346	> Let `v~ ∈ v_0'` be a component of `L̃_∞` over `v_0` which is not a
   347	> dicritical-incident block. Then **every** one of the `m(v~)` points of `v~`
   348	> over `p` is a mark, `F|_{v~}` is unramified over `p`, and each of those marks
   349	> has index `e_y = n(v~)`. Consequently the ramification profile of
   350	> `E~-bar -> P^1` over the infinity puncture is exactly the multiset
   351	> ```text
   352	>          { n(v~) repeated m(v~) times :  v~ ∈ v_0' non-dicritical } ,
   353	> ```
   354	> a partition of `a`.
   355	>
   356	> *Proof.* By DF-2 at `v_0`, `sum_{v~ ∈ v_0'} Deg v~ = N`; the dicritical blocks
   357	> contribute `sum_l s_l mu_l = W` (DF-2 at `g` is BI-1; the block incident to
   358	> `l` carries `Deg = s_l mu_l`, DR Lemma 6 / Prop 1), so the non-dicritical
   359	> components carry `sum Deg v~ = N - W = a`. Each `v~` has at most `m(v~)`
   360	> points over `p`; writing `c_{v~}` for the number that are marks and using
   361	> BI-2, `sum_{v~} c_{v~} n(v~) = a` by BI-3 and `(H-∞)`. Since
   362	> `c_{v~} <= m(v~)` and `sum_{v~} m(v~) n(v~) = a`, every inequality is an
   363	> equality: `c_{v~} = m(v~)` for all `v~`. ∎
   364	
   365	**Determinants of the resulting branches.** Feed BI-5 into DF-4 and DF-7.
   366	
   367	> **COROLLARY BI-6 (the coprimality load carried by `E`).** A mark-carrying
   368	> component with `Deg = 1` (a *unit lift*, `m = n = 1`) has transfer factor
   369	> `n/Deg = 1`, so by DF-4 its source branch determinants **equal** the
   370	> corresponding target branch determinants. Hence two unit lifts of `E` may
   371	> merge at a source fork only over a target branch of determinant `1`: over a
   372	> branch with `det B > 1` they would present two equal non-unit branch
   373	> determinants at one source vertex, contradicting DF-7 (Lemma 2). With DF-6
   374	> (`det R_{v_0} = 1`, `det D_{v_0} > 1`, `det L_{v_0} > 1`) the merges of
   375	> `E`-unit-lifts are confined to the `R`-side.
   376	
   377	This is the mechanism DR uses over and over ("*repeats a non-unit determinant
   378	and fails coprimality*", DR:379, 391, 448) — and BI-6 says what it is *about*:
   379	**the coprimality kills in DO I are kills of the multiplicity of `E`'s sheets.**
   380	That is the first structural statement linking the two halves BE §6 called
   381	disjoint.
   382	
   383	### 3.5 A GAP-CANDIDATE raised against the block the campaign would lean on
   384	
   385	The instrument uses only `Deg B_l = s_l mu_l`. The `(m,n)` **split** of that
   386	block is *not* determined by the frozen replay, and my independent local
   387	computation disagrees with the reading DR's §6 assembly presupposes:
   388	
   389	```text
   390	 Local computation at p̃_l (coordinates as in BI-3, assuming p̃_l is a
   391	 transverse crossing of l with a single component B_l of L̃_∞ and F is monomial
   392	 there):   n(B_l) = ord_{B_l} F^*(v_0) = a_1 = index of F|_l at p̃_l = s_l ,
   393	           m(B_l) >= b_2 = mu_l ,     so   (m,n)(B_l) = (mu_l, s_l).
   394	 DR's §6 assembly instead treats the object incident to g~ at N=4 as a
   395	 CONSTANT block, i.e. m = 1, n = 2, i.e. (m,n) = (s_l, mu_l): the TRANSPOSE.
   396	 Both give Deg = s_l mu_l = 2.  They differ on whether that object is a FORK.
   397	```
   398	
   399	If `(m,n)(B_l) = (mu_l, s_l)` and `mu_l >= 2` (always, 7.B'), then `B_l` is a
   400	fork of degree `s_l mu_l`; at `N = 4` that is a **degree-two fork**, which DR's
   401	Corollary 4 excludes — a one-line kill, which the length of DO I refutes. The
   402	resolution is visible in the MEASURED model of §3.2: **there, the component the
   403	dicritical meets at `p̃` is CONTRACTED**, so `B_l` is not the physically adjacent
   404	component and DR's Prop 1 transfers across a contracted tail. Hence:
   405	
   406	```text
