   184	## 2. Meridional Rank And The Group Audit
   185	
   186	The meridian proof in DG:312-328 and DR:235-250 is a proof about a group
   187	generation number. A generic line sees `n=#(L cap A_F)` meridians, and these
   188	generate `G=pi_1(C^2\A_F)`. Define `mrk(G)` to be the least number of
   189	conjugates of a meridian needed to generate `G`. Then
   190	
   191	```text
   192	mrk(G) <= n.
   193	```
   194	
   195	The block-merge argument lower-bounds `mrk(G)` by
   196	`ceil((N-1)/(W-S))`; only after that inequality do we get a lower bound on
   197	`n`. This is a theorem about the stated apparatus. It is not, as written at
   198	MFS:82-83, a theorem that no future group-theoretic idea could ever interact
   199	with geometry to upper-bound `n`. The safe replacement is:
   200	
   201	```text
   202	Any argument whose only bridge to the curve degree is mrk(G)<=n can yield only
   203	a lower bound on n.
   204	```
